@ 17.7.2.1 - West Germanic to Anglo-Frisian
# citation: Siride? & Pogostick Man, the latter citing Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Anglo-Frisian languages”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Anglo-Frisian_languages>; Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Old Frisian”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Old_Frisian>; and Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Old English phonology”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Old_English_phonology>
	a → ɑ̃ / _N (short only)
	VN → Ṽː / _F
	a → æː / short only, includes diphthongs ! B or *ã in next syllable
	k ɡ → tʃ j(?)
	æː → aː “under to [sic] the influence of neighboring consonants”, but the article doesn’t elaborate
	æː → eː
	æu → au (æ → a / _B in general?)
	aː → æː / ! _N or if nasalized
	i o → e a / [- stress]
	ai au eu → {eː,aː} aː ia
	ia iu → jaː juː
	a → æ / ! _N or if nasalized, or if *B or *ã in next syllable
	h → ∅ / V_V
	{i,u} → ∅ / -# ! VC_
